0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 81 Tm /F1 10 Tf 13 TL (This report documents an end-to-end Retrieval-Augmented Generation \(RAG\) pipeline built for the) Tj T* (MTH-424 project. The system indexes the Project Gutenberg text #2347 \("The Adventure of the Dying) Tj T* (Detective"\) in Milvus, retrieves relevant passages for each question from the NarrativeQA test set, and) Tj T* (generates an answer with a small instruction-tuned LLM. Two chunking strategies were implemented) Tj T* (and compared across multiple parameter sweeps. The best configuration achieved ROUGE-L=0.2391) Tj T* (and BLEU=0.0306 \(average=0.1348\), exceeding the project thresholds \(ROUGE-L>=0.05,) Tj T* (BLEU>=0.01\).) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 467.1969 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 4 Tm /F2 18 Tf 22 TL (1. Methodology) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 240.1969 cm
q
BT 1 0 0 1 0 211 Tm 13 TL /F2 10 Tf 0 0 0 rg (Data sources.) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( The system indexes the Project Gutenberg text #2347 \("The Adventure of the Dying) Tj T* (Detective"\) and evaluates on question-answer pairs from the test split of the deepmind/narrativeqa) Tj T* (dataset filtered to reference the same Gutenberg source.) Tj  T* /F2 10 Tf (Data cleaning.) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( The Gutenberg header/footer are removed, whitespace is normalized, and early) Tj T* (boilerplate is trimmed by starting the story at the first occurrence of "Mrs. Hudson" to avoid front-matter) Tj T* (leaking into the initial chunks.) Tj  T* /F2 10 Tf (Embeddings and indexing.) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( Chunks are embedded with BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5 using query/passage) Tj T* (instruction prefixes and normalized embeddings. Two Milvus collections are built \(one per chunking) Tj T* (method\) using inner product similarity \(cosine-like under normalization\).) Tj  T* /F2 10 Tf (Retrieval and reranking.) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( For each question, the system retrieves RETRIEVE_K=80 candidates from) Tj T* (Milvus and \(when available\) reranks them using BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3. The top TOP_K chunks are) Tj T* (concatenated \(up to 12,000 characters\) to form the context supplied to the generator.) Tj  T* /F2 10 Tf (Generation.) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( The generator is Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct \(with one experiment on) Tj T* (Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\). Decoding is deterministic \(do_sample=False, temperature=0.0\). A) Tj T* (post-processing step keeps at most two sentences to stabilize ROUGE/BLEU scores.) Tj  T* /F2 10 Tf (Evaluation.) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( ROUGE-L \(with stemming\) and corpus BLEU \(with smoothing\) are computed against the) Tj T* (gold answers; all reported scores use four decimals.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 214.1969 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 4 Tm /F2 14 Tf 18 TL (1.1 Chunking strategies) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 68.3622 65.19685 cm
q
BT 1 0 0 1 0 133 Tm 13 TL /F2 10 Tf 0 0 0 rg (Chunker A: fixed-length character chunking.) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( The cleaned story is split into windows of 1,200) Tj T* (characters with a configurable character overlap \(A\). This method is simple and fast, but it can cut) Tj T* (across sentence boundaries and may reduce the coherence of individual chunks when boundaries) Tj T* (occur mid-sentence.) Tj  T* /F2 10 Tf (Chunker B: structure-aware chunking.) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( Chunks are formed by accumulating linguistic units until the) Tj T* (same 1,200-character budget is reached. Two variants were tested: \(i\) sentence-based chunking with) Tj T* (B-sentence overlap, and \(ii\) paragraph-based chunking with B-paragraph overlap. These approaches) Tj T* (preserve discourse structure, which can improve retrieval relevance and reduce boundary artifacts, at) Tj T* (the cost of slightly more preprocessing.) Tj  T* /F2 10 Tf (Expected outcome \(theory\).) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( I expected Chunker B to outperform Chunker A because semantically) Tj T* (coherent chunks should match queries more precisely, and the overlap reduces the risk that a key fact) Tj T* ET
Q
